<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="IQ8" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction Questions</title>
<ol>
<li>
<p> 
   A trophic level is the position that an organism occupies in a food chain. Draw a food chain of the insect–plant community described in the Phenomenon that illustrates the flow of energy through four trophic levels. Use an arrow between each organism in the chain to indicate the direction of energy flow (i.e., an arrow from the food source to the feeder).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
    P. bicornis ants, clerid beetles, Piper plants, and herbivorous insects were sampled from two sites in a lowland tropical forest in Costa Rica. The table shows the relative density (High vs. Low) of individuals for all trophic levels in the Piper plant system previously described in the laboratory introduction.
</p>
<li>
<p>
What interactions might explain differences between the relative densities of organisms sampled at Site 1?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p> 
Is the relationship between the relative density of the clerid beetle population and that of the Piper plant population at Site 1 a direct or indirect relationship? Use evidence to support your claim.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The relative density of clerid beetles is low at Site 2. Fill out the column of Site 2 in the table with your predictions for the relative densities of organisms at all other trophic levels
</p>
</li>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Exotic species are organisms introduced deliberately or accidentally into a habitat to which they are not native. Others, called invasive species, expand their range with increasing ecological, economic, and environmental consequences. Predict what may happen to the size of the P. bicornis ant population if an exotic species of plant is introduced into the lowland tropical forests of Costa Rica with very similar resource requirements as the native Piper plant. Assume that this exotic plant reproduces rapidly and demonstrates a faster growth rate than the native Piper plants.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The lowland tropical forest community described in the Phenomenon is part of a highly dynamic ecosystem consisting of both biotic (living) and abiotic (nonliving) components. Many of these components work together to maintain stability, but ecosystems do change under certain circumstances. It is very important for ecologists to understand what leads to stability and what leads to change in an ecosystem if they are going to protect and conserve these systems. What factors result in ecosystem change? Make an initial claim regarding the factors that influence ecosystems both positively and negatively.
</p>
</li>
